<section><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item colspan="27" vertical-align="center"><p role="eyebrow">Print · historical evidence</p><h2>Print stabilizes space</h2><p>Movable type makes a composed page repeatable. In this Gutenberg Bible page, two columns and a strong text hierarchy organize a fixed reading surface.</p><p>The reader follows a sequence already arranged on the page. Production combines repeatable type with the material character of paper and ornament.</p><p><b>Design question:</b> what must stay stable across many copies?</p><p role="caption"><a href="https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2006680004/">Library of Congress · cph.3a51868</a></p></grid-item><grid-item colspan="33" vertical-align="center"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/agent-uploads/HZgmUAeVC4pz8Wr__MP82.jpg" source="image.upload" height="320pt" alt="Gutenberg Bible page. Library of Congress, Prints &amp; Photographs Division, cph.3a51868. Historical page reproduction."></img><p font-size="12pt" role="caption">Gutenberg Bible page. Library of Congress, Prints &amp; Photographs Division, cph.3a51868. Historical page reproduction.</p></grid-item></grid></section>